// Problem: Remove Outermost Parentheses
// LeetCode: 1021

// Why this code is needed:
// In a valid parentheses string, primitive parentheses contain an outermost pair
// which does not contribute to the actual nested structure.
// This code removes only those outermost brackets while keeping all inner brackets.

// Approach:
// Use a depth counter to track the nesting level.
// If '(' is not the outermost opening bracket, add it to answer.
// If ')' is not the outermost closing bracket, add it to answer.

class Solution {
public:
    string removeOuterParentheses(string s) {
        string ans = "";
        int depth = 0;

        for(char ch : s) {
            if(ch == '(') {
                if(depth > 0) {
                    ans.push_back(ch);
                }
                depth++;
            }
            else {
                depth--;
                if(depth > 0) {
                    ans.push_back(ch);
                }
            }
        }

        return ans;
    }
};

/*
TIME COMPLEXITY: O(n)
SPACE COMPLEXITY: O(n)

Mistakes:
1. Tried modifying the original string using erase(), which caused index shifting problems.
2. Used substr() incorrectly because C++ substr() takes length, not ending index.
3. Initially handled each primitive separately, which made the logic more complex.
4. Did not consider that depth itself can identify outermost parentheses.

Learning:
1. Depth tracking is useful for problems involving nested parentheses.
2. Opening bracket at depth 0 and closing bracket returning to depth 0 are outermost brackets.
3. Building the answer separately is safer and cleaner than modifying the input string.
4. Many stack-based problems can be solved using only a counter when only balance matters.

Possible Optimization:
The solution already uses a single traversal, which is the optimal O(n) approach.
*/
